@* D-830 — the one implementation of "render this control only if the signed-in
   user holds this permission".

   It lives in the shared component library rather than the Control Panel because
   SimfDataGrid renders its own toolbar, row and context-menu buttons: a page
   cannot wrap them from the outside, so the gate has to be reachable from inside
   the grid. The Control Panel's <AuthorizedAction> is now a thin alias over this
   component, so page markup is unchanged and there is still exactly one place
   where a permission code becomes a policy decision.

   Permission = null  -> transparent: the content renders, unconditionally. This
   is the default on every parameter, so adding the gate cannot change a screen
   that has not opted in.

   Permission = ""    -> gated, and refused. PolicyFor("") names no registered
   policy, so AuthorizeView denies. That is the behaviour <AuthorizedAction> has
   always had for an empty code, and it is deliberately NOT treated as "ungated" —
   a blank permission must never widen access.

   This is a usability layer, not the security boundary: the API enforces the same
   permission on the endpoint each button calls. *@
@namespace SIMF.Components.Forms

@if (Permission is null)
{
    @ChildContent
}
else
{
    <AuthorizeView Policy="@PermissionCatalog.PolicyFor(Permission)">
        <Authorized>@ChildContent</Authorized>
    </AuthorizeView>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The permission code that gates the content, e.g.
    /// <c>PermissionCatalog.Sessions.Edit</c>. Null renders the content ungated.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Permission { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
